+
+### Installation correction
+
+`verify_install.verify()` resolved each resource with `Path.resolve()` but
+compared it against the caller's unresolved installation prefix. A valid
+installation reached through a directory alias could therefore be rejected
+before checksum verification. The command-line entry already resolved its
+argument; the direct call from `tests/cli/install.py` did not.
+
+The correction resolves the prefix inside `verify()` and extracts the unchanged
+inventory/checksum loop as `verify_native_resources()`, which also canonicalizes
+its input. The fixed namespace comparison retains the required inventory
+header, minimum entries, installation-relative token, containment, complete
+SHA-256 verification and missing-file failures. No Runtime path policy changed.
+
+Actual reproduction executed the baseline's inventory loop and the corrected
+helper against identical bytes. Both `void_runtime.lib` and `libvoid_runtime.a`
+failed the baseline containment check through a dot-traversal alias and passed
+with the correction. A native Windows `GetShortPathNameW` alias also reproduced
+both failures: the same installation was addressed through a real 8.3 path and
+its long spelling. The corrected verifier passed both. POSIX symlink aliases
+were exercised on Linux. Native macOS execution is unavailable; these Linux
+checks do not establish macOS acceptance. The exact original hosted prefix
+spelling was not printed by the failing test, so it is not invented here.
+
+`tests/cli/install_resources.py`, registered as `cli_install_resources`, adds
+alias, `.lib`/`.a`, checksum tamper, missing resource, traversal and symlink-
+escape regressions, plus the real Windows short-path case. Linux: six passed,
+one Windows-only skip. Windows 10: five passed, two symlink-privilege skips;
+the real short-path case passed. No original assertion or negative control was
+removed.
+
+### Windows process investigation: unresolved hosted cause
+
+Both completed Server 2022 jobs failed `explicit child launch`; their source
+fixtures failed at `convert.unwrap(process.start(...))`. The original logs,
+portable JSON and CTest XML omit the failing `Result.kind`, Win32 code and
+operation stage. No exact Server code can be recovered from those records.
+The failing command paths were parsed from both artifacts: ordinary single
+backslashes, absolute drive paths and resolved temporary working directories;
+no doubled-separator defect was found.
+
+The existing Windows 10 guest (build19045, VM display name “Windows 11”) passed
+both original portable binaries: 98 checks per profile with a controlled private
+TMP/TEMP. An initial probe using its ordinary Temp directory failed private-root
+admission; that diagnostic remains and no ACL was weakened. The existing
+protected task Temp was then used, as in the earlier qualification.
+Native metadata probes succeeded with both `FILE_LIST_DIRECTORY` alone and with
+`FILE_READ_ATTRIBUTES` on all tested directories. This does not prove how
+Server 2022 behaves and does not justify assigning its failure to that access
+mask. No ACL or inherited-handle cause is asserted without evidence.
+
+No production launch-policy correction has been inferred.
+`host_process_windows.cpp` now attaches a private stage ordinal to the existing
+`Result.detail` slot on native exceptions. The original portable kind and native
+code remain intact; public `system_error` shape and success replies are unchanged.
+Native/guard calls and their arguments compare identically to baseline.
+Stages1–13 identify paths, arguments, environment, image ancestors, cwd ancestors,
+image open/metadata, cwd open/metadata, stdio, attribute init/update and process
+creation. This is diagnostic metadata, not a Server repair.
+The native fixture now prints `Result.kind`/native code on failure and performs
+read-only image-ancestor, working-directory-ancestor and standard-handle probes.
+The source fixture prints its portable error code and `os_code` before the
+original failing unwrap. All launch, exact exit status, Unicode/literal argument,
+environment, ownership and negative assertions remain.
+A deliberate missing-executable probe in each native profile emitted
+`process.start diagnostic 1 2`, then failed as expected. That is a controlled
+not-found diagnostic, **not the unknown Server failure code**.
+
+Capturing the actual hosted error and stage requires Server 2022 execution of
+these diagnostics. No such local host is available and dispatching CI is
+explicitly prohibited. A Windows 10 pass cannot resolve this gap. The process
+repair remains incomplete; these diagnostics are not represented as a fix.
